{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You shall not do so to HaShem your Elohim.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said in the Sifre, and this is their language: Rabbi Yishmael said — whence do we learn concerning one who erases a letter from the [divine] Name that he transgresses a negative commandment? For it is written: ״וְאִבַּדְתֶּם אֶת שְׁמָם / לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן כֵּן״ — end quote. Now perhaps the verse intends to allude to two specific points of law that exist in the law of erasing [the divine] Name from among His names, may He be blessed: one is the Name itself, and one is that which is appended to the Name of letters following it. In saying ״לַה׳״ (to HaShem), behold, this is a warning concerning the Name — and not the Tetragrammaton alone, but every name like it among His names, and this serves as a general principle for them. And the saying of <b>your Elohim</b> alludes to the suffix כ״ם appended to Elohim. And what the Rambam wrote in chapter 6 of Hilkhot Yesodei ha-Torah, that one is not liable to lashes except for the letters of the [Tetragrammaton] Name — since the matter is not explicitly spelled out in the verse, one does not receive lashes for it. And there in the Sifre they further said, and this is their language: Whence do we learn concerning one who dislodges a single stone from the Temple courtyard that he transgresses a negative commandment? For it is written: ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן״ etc. It appears that he did not say precisely 'a single stone' but rather [any case] where his act of dislodging makes an impression on the structure."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:51.891832557Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}